WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:02.400
You may have heard
the expression

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:04.240
“knowledge is power.”

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:07.040
Well, today we’re going
to give you more power

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:08.800
to control your
diet and lifestyle

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:10.960
by giving you the facts.

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:13.760
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:16.240
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:18.720
Today we take a critical look

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:22.400
at one of the foods people
have trouble cutting down on.

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:26.640
We start with the simple question:
is cheese really bad for you?

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:30.320
In a series of videos I did
about saturated fat,

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:33.360
I talked about a major campaign
launched by the global dairy industry

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:36.720
to neutralize the negative image
of milkfat among regulators

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:39.840
and health professionals
as related to heart disease.

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:43.760
That campaign continues to
this day with the publication of

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:48.400
a meta-analysis demonstrating neutral,
meaning non-harmful associations,

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:51.680
between dairy products and
cardiovascular disease and death.

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:55.600
OK, well first of all, how do
we know the dairy industry

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:57.280
had anything to do with this study?

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:00.400
Well, it was published in a
journal that forces authors

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:02.800
to disclose financial
conflicts of interest.

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:08.160
Let's see, what they divulged.
Dairy, dairy, dairy, dairy,

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:10.880
dairy, dairy, the 4th largest
dairy company in the world,

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:14.960
dairy, dairy, milk, beer, soda,
McDonald's, dairy, dairy,

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:16.560
dairy, dairy, dairy.

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:21.280
Oh, and the study itself was explicitly
funded by dairy, dairy, dairy.

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:22.880
OK, then.

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:27.120
The other big new one was this,
suggesting that a little bit of cheese

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:29.360
every day isn't just neutral,
but actually good for you.

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:33.440
And they make it clear that they
have no conflicts of interest;

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:36.000
they're just employees of
the Yili Innovation Center

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:41.600
and the Yili R&amp;D Center, you know,
China's largest dairy producer,

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:45.120
making it one of the world's
largest dairy companies.

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:51.600
OK, but how can cheese consumption be
associated with better health outcomes?

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:53.760
Well, most of these studies
were from Europe,

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:58.160
where cheese consumption is associated
with a higher socioeconomic status.

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:01.120
See, in Europe they're not eating
Cheez Whiz and Velveeta.

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:04.720
Cheese is generally an expensive
product, and so who eats cheese?

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:06.640
Those with higher paying jobs,

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:10.240
higher socioeconomic strata,
higher education levels,

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:13.520
all of which are associated
with better health outcomes,

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:16.080
which may have nothing to do
with their cheese consumption.

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:19.200
Higher socioeconomic
groups also consume

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:22.720
more fruits and vegetables,
and more candies.

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:25.040
So I bet you could
do a population study

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:28.320
and show candy consumption is
associated with better health.

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:31.680
Shh, don't tell the National
Confectioner's Association.

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:32.960
Too late!

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:36.880
Did you know that candy consumers
have lower levels of inflammation,

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:39.920
a 14% decreased risk
of elevated blood pressure?

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:45.200
Brought to you by the candy industry
and the USDA, our government,

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:49.920
which props up the sugar industry
to the tune of a billion dollars a year.

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:53.200
It's like when our tax dollars
are used to buy up surplus cheese.

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:55.920
Paul Shapiro wrote a great editorial.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:00.400
Imagine the headline "Government
Buys $20 Million in Surplus Pepsi,"

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:04.320
our hard-earned tax dollars buying
up millions of unwanted cola cans,

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:06.960
all as a favor to the
flailing soda industry,

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:09.840
which just kept producing
drinks no one wanted.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:13.600
As outrageous as such a government
handout to the soda industry would be,

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:18.240
that's exactly what the USDA
is doing for the dairy industry.

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:21.680
Michele Simon did a great
report on how our government

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:25.680
colludes with the industry
to promote dairy junk foods.

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:28.160
The federal government administers
"check-off programs"

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:30.080
to promote milk and dairy.

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:33.360
McDonald's has six dedicated dairy
checkoff program employees

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:37.520
at its corporate headquarters to
try to squeeze in more cheese.

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:40.320
That's how we got
double steak quesadillas.

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:43.200
That's how we got 3-Cheese
Stuffed Crust Pizza,

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:46.320
complete with a
"Summer of Cheese."

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:49.040
These funds are being
used to promote foods

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:50.960
that contribute to
the very diseases

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:53.280
our federal government is
allegedly trying to prevent.

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:55.760
Does it make sense
to tell Americans

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:58.080
to avoid foods high in salt,
sugar, and saturated fat,

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:02.400
while engaging in the promotion
of those same foods?

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:07.120
Look, the meat and dairy industries can
do what they like with their own money,

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:10.720
but the public power of taxation
should be used for the public good,

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:13.840
not to support the dairy
and candy industries.

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:16.960
In our next story, we compare
dairy to other foods

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:19.122
for heart attack
and stroke risk.

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:23.280
When industry-funded studies
suggest their products

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:27.600
have neutral health effects,
or even beneficial,

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:30.960
one question you always have
to ask is compared to what?

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:33.920
Is cheese healthy?
Compared to what?

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:37.680
If you're sitting down to make a
sandwich, cheese is probably healthy—

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:41.840
compared to bologna—but compared
to peanut butter? No way.

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:46.480
That's the point Walt Willet made,
former chair of nutrition at Harvard.

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:49.520
To conclude that dairy foods are
"neutral" could be misleading, as

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:52.800
it could be misinterpreted to mean that
increasing consumption of dairy foods

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:56.000
would have no effects on
cardiovascular disease or mortality.

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:57.840
Lost is that the health
effects of increasing

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:00.720
or decreasing consumption
of dairy foods would depend

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:05.600
importantly on the specific foods
that are substituted for dairy foods.

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:07.680
Like what are you going
to put on your salad?

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:12.800
Cheese would be healthy compared
to bacon, but not compared to nuts.

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:15.440
See, consumption of
nuts or plant protein

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:17.920
has been found to be
protectively associated

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:20.880
with the risk of coronary heart
disease and type 2 diabetes.

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:24.480
In contrast, intake of red meat,
for example, has been associated

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:25.600
with increased risk.

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:29.760
Thus, it's reasonable to assume that
the lack of association with dairy foods

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:33.200
could put them somewhere in the
middle of a spectrum of healthfulness,

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:36.880
but certainly not an optimal
source of energy or protein.

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:39.920
More broadly, the available
evidence supports policies

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:42.800
that limit dairy production
and encourages production

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:45.360
of healthier sources
of proteins and fats.

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:48.000
He wasn't just speculating.

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:51.680
This was based on three famous Harvard
studies involving hundreds of thousands

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:55.040
of men and women exceeding
5 million person-years of follow-up.

00:05:55.760 --> 00:06:00.240
This was really the first large-scale
prospective study to examine dairy fat

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:04.720
intake, compared to other types of fat in
relation to heart attack and stroke risk.

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:07.760
So replacing like 100 calories
of fat worth of cheese

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:10.960
with 100 calories of fat worth
of peanut butter on a daily basis

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:12.880
might reduce risk up to 24%,

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:17.920
whereas substitution with other
animal fats might make things worse.

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:21.600
Here's how it breaks
down for heart disease.

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:24.640
Swapping dairy fat for like
vegetable oil would be associated

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:30.400
with a decrease in disease risk, whereas
swapping dairy for meat increases risk.

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:35.440
Dairy fat calories may be as
bad or worse as straight sugar.

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:41.120
The lowest risk would entail swapping
to a whole plant food, like whole grains.

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:44.320
Yeah, dairy products
are a major contributor

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:47.360
to the saturated fat in the diet
and have thus been targeted as

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:51.440
one of the main dietary causes of, you
know, the #1 killer of men and women.

00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:54.160
But the dairy industry
likes to argue

00:06:54.160 --> 00:06:57.520
there are other things in dairy products
like fermentation by-products

00:06:57.520 --> 00:07:01.040
in cheese that could counteract
the saturated fat effects,

00:07:01.600 --> 00:07:05.600
all part of an explicit campaign by
the dairy industry to neutralize

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:08.720
the negative image of milkfat among
regulators and health professionals.

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:13.760
If Global Dairy Platform looks familiar,
they were one of the funders

00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:16.000
of the milk-and-dairy-is-neutral study,

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:18.880
trotting out their dairy-fat-
is-counteracted notion.

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:22.400
To which the American Heart
Association responds that

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:24.960
no information from controlled
studies supports the assertion

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:27.760
that fermentation adds
beneficial nutrients to cheese

00:07:27.760 --> 00:07:30.880
that somehow counteract the
harmful effects of the saturated fat.

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:34.480
We need to cut down on
dairy, meat, coconut oil,

00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:37.120
no matter what their
respective industries say.

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:40.320
In fact, that's the reason the
American Heart Association

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:44.480
felt that they needed to release this
special Presidential Advisory in 2017.

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:48.560
They wanted to set the record straight
on why well-conducted scientific research

00:07:48.560 --> 00:07:53.452
overwhelmingly supports limiting
saturated fat in the diet.

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:57.040
Finally today, we look at how
the meat and dairy industries

00:07:57.040 --> 00:07:59.360
design studies showing
their products have neutral

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:03.440
or even beneficial effects on
cholesterol and inflammation.

00:08:04.293 --> 00:08:07.280
But what about
this interventional study?

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:10.480
A randomized, crossover trial which
compared a high-fat cheese diet,

00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:13.280
to a high-fat meat diet,
to a low-fat diet.

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:16.640
A high cheese diet: CHEESE,
which is loaded with saturated fat,

00:08:16.640 --> 00:08:20.160
a high meat diet: MEAT, which
is loaded with saturated fat,

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:22.640
versus CARB, a low-fat diet,

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:26.720
and people ended up with
the same cholesterol levels.

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:29.920
Let's see how they did it.

00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:33.120
Half the study was paid for
in part by the dairy industry,

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:36.240
and the other half paid for by
dairy, dairy, dairy, and dairy.

00:08:37.040 --> 00:08:40.160
If you're the dairy industry, and
you're trying to design a study

00:08:40.160 --> 00:08:43.840
to show that a high cheese diet
doesn't raise cholesterol,

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:45.769
how would you go
about doing that?

00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:50.160
The beef industry was in the
same pickle as the cheese industry.

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:52.880
Beef has saturated fat,
which raises cholesterol,

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:55.440
which raises the risk of
dying from our #1 killer.

00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:57.600
What's an industry to do?

00:08:58.240 --> 00:09:04.601
So they designed a study where they
added beef, and cholesterols went down.

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:05.974
How is that possible?

00:09:06.320 --> 00:09:12.240
They did this by cutting out so much
dairy, pork, poultry, fish, and eggs

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:15.760
that their overall saturated
fat intake was cut in half.

00:09:16.480 --> 00:09:20.640
They cut saturated fat levels in half
and the cholesterol levels went down.

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:22.320
Well, duh.

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:24.080
They could
have swapped in Twinkies

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:28.720
and said snack cakes lower your
cholesterol, or frosting or anything.

00:09:29.520 --> 00:09:32.000
OK, so now that 
you know the trick,

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:34.640
let's go back to this study.

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:37.440
How are you going to get 
a high-fat cheese diet,

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:39.040
and a high-fat meat diet

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:41.920
to have anywhere near the 
same saturated fat level

00:09:41.920 --> 00:09:45.120
as a diet with neither, unless...

00:09:45.120 --> 00:09:46.480
Wait, don't tell me.

00:09:46.480 --> 00:09:49.840
What, they added coconut oil 
or something to the other diet?

00:09:50.560 --> 00:09:53.200
They added so much 
coconut oil and cookies

00:09:53.200 --> 00:09:54.800
to the so-called low fat diet

00:09:54.800 --> 00:09:57.920
that they were able to sufficiently 
raise the level of saturated fat

00:09:57.920 --> 00:10:00.240
to cause a similar 
rise in cholesterol.

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:03.280
That's now you can make sure
 a cheese or meat-rich diet

00:10:03.280 --> 00:10:05.600
look like it doesn't
raise cholesterol.

00:10:06.560 --> 00:10:09.360
That reminds me of the desperation

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:12.000
evident in this study
that compared the effects

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:15.680
of dairy cheddar cheese to a nondairy
cheddar cheese called Daiya.

00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:18.880
Milk consumption has
plummeted in recent years

00:10:18.880 --> 00:10:21.280
as people have discovered
plant-based alternatives

00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:22.560
like soymilk and almond milk,

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:26.080
and now there's plant-based
cheese alternatives?

00:10:26.080 --> 00:10:28.960
What's the National Dairy Council to do?

00:10:29.680 --> 00:10:33.680
How are you going to design a study
that shows it's healthier to eat cheese,

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:39.200
design a study where cheese causes less
inflammation than the vegan alternative?

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:41.600
They got their work
cut out for them.

00:10:42.640 --> 00:10:44.400
Daiya is no health
food by any stretch,

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:47.920
but definitely three times less
saturated fat than cow cheese.

00:10:48.800 --> 00:10:50.240
So, I give up.

00:10:50.240 --> 00:10:53.280
How could you possibly show
more inflammation from Daiya?

00:10:54.480 --> 00:10:58.640
Well, there is one fat that may cause
more inflammation that milk fat: palm oil.

00:10:58.640 --> 00:11:00.240
In fact, it may raise
cholesterol levels

00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:03.760
as much as trans-fat laden
partially hydrogenated oil.

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:08.000
Yeah, but what, are you telling me
they like slipped the Daiya group

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:09.680
some extra palm oil on the side?

00:11:10.240 --> 00:11:11.760
Yes, can you believe it?

00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:16.000
They compared cheese
to Daiya plus palm oil,

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:18.640
so much extra palm oil that the
vegan alternative meal ended up

00:11:18.640 --> 00:11:21.680
having the same amount of
saturated fat as the cheese meal.

00:11:21.680 --> 00:11:25.760
That's like proving tofu is worse
than beef by doing a study

00:11:25.760 --> 00:11:29.920
where they compared a beef burger
to a tofu patty stuffed with lard.

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:35.280
Oh, wait, the meat industry
already did that, but at least

00:11:35.280 --> 00:11:36.960
they had the decency to concede

00:11:36.960 --> 00:11:40.240
that replacement of meat
by tofu in the habitual diet

00:11:40.240 --> 00:11:46.085
would probably not usually be
accompanied by the addition of lard.

